{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "strict": true,
    "noUncheckedIndexedAccess": true,
    // Dead declarations are how a refactor leaves a second, stale copy of live logic
    // behind — App.tsx once carried a 14-line duplicate of the .ork flight-data writer
    // that nothing called, and a fix applied to the wrong copy is invisible. Nothing
    // else in the toolchain reported unused declarations before these were turned on:
    // vitest transpiles without checking. ESLint's @typescript-eslint/no-unused-vars
    // now covers the same ground in the .ts source too, and is the only check that
    // reaches the .mjs build scripts tsc never reads. Params deliberately left unused
    // are exempted the way the source already does it — prefix with `_`.
    "noUnusedLocals": true,
    "noUnusedParameters": true,
    // The free half of the 2026-09-08 audit's tooling recommendation: every flag
    // below was MEASURED at zero violations against the tree it was added to
    // (`npx tsc -p tsconfig.json --noEmit --<flag>`), so each is a pure ratchet
    // — it costs nothing today and refuses the next instance. They are grouped
    // because that measurement is the reason they are here, and a future reader
    // deciding whether to keep one should re-measure rather than assume.
    //
    // noImplicitReturns is the load-bearing one for THIS app: a branch that
    // silently returns undefined where a number is expected is how a wrong
    // number reaches the screen, which is the defect class the whole audit was
    // about. noFallthroughCasesInSwitch guards the format readers' switch
    // chains, where a dropped `break` would mis-read a component type.
    "noImplicitOverride": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,
    "noImplicitReturns": true,
    "allowUnreachableCode": false,
    // The 2026-09-22 audit's Step A, measured the same way (0 errors each, at
    // 3918947). allowUnusedLabels: `(n) => { cd: n }` is a block holding a
    // label, so the arrow meant to return an object returns undefined.
    // noUncheckedSideEffectImports: a bare `import './x'` whose path does not
    // resolve is otherwise NOT an error.
    // erasableSyntaxOnly: no enum/namespace/parameter property, so every .ts file
    // stays strippable by any tool that only erases types.
    //
    // verbatimModuleSyntax was declined on 2026-09-08 because it is the one flag
    // with EMIT semantics and --noEmit does not exercise them. Re-decided on that
    // ground: it IS live at emit here (Vite 5 hands it to esbuild, which then keeps
    // an import it would otherwise have dropped — probed with transformWithEsbuild),
    // and with it on, `npm run build` produced a dist/ (every chunk, sw.js and its
    // precache revisions) and an engine dist/ byte-identical to the build without
    // it. The one import shape it would have changed, `import { type X }` with
    // every name type-only (tree/shroudMesh.ts), now reads `import type` — under
    // this flag the other form keeps a bare side-effect import. What it buys: tsc
    // refuses an import of a type without `type`, so what a file imports is what
    // esbuild and vitest load, rather than whatever elision decides.
    //
    // Declined the same day, on its count: exactOptionalPropertyTypes (62 errors
    // in the shipped source's project, packages/app/tsconfig.app.json, and 101 in
    // the test project). That is a migration, not a ratchet.
    // noPropertyAccessFromIndexSignature is on for the shipped source only (0
    // there, 11 in tests): see packages/app/tsconfig.app.json.
    "allowUnusedLabels": false,
    "noUncheckedSideEffectImports": true,
    "erasableSyntaxOnly": true,
    "verbatimModuleSyntax": true,
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true,
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "isolatedModules": true
  }
}
